%I A401927 #11 Nov 21 2026 00:13:59
%S A401927 1,2,6,12,6,12,30,60,180,360,180,360,30,60,180,360,180,360,30,60,180,
%T A401927 360,180,360,30,60,180,360,180,360,210,420,1260,2520,1260,2520,6300,
%U A401927 12600,37800,75600,37800,75600,6300,12600,37800,75600,37800,75600,6300,12600,37800,75600,37800,75600,6300,12600,37800,75600
%N A401927 a(n) = A108951(A328571(n)), where A108951 is fully multiplicative with a(p) = p# (A034386), and A328571(n) = A007947(A276086(n)), i.e., the squarefree kernel of the primorial base exp-function.
%C A401927 Form product of the radix primes of all nonzero digits in the primorial base expansion of n (A049345), then take the primorial inflation of that. See example.
%H A401927 Antti Karttunen, <a href="/A401927/b401927.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..16170</a>
%H A401927 <a href="/index/Pri#primorialbase">Index entries for sequences related to primorial base</a>.
%H A401927 <a href="/index/Pri#primorial_numbers">Index entries for sequences related to primorial numbers</a>.
%F A401927 a(n) = A108951(Product_{p|A276086(n), p prime} p) = Product_{p|A276086(n), p prime} A034386(p).
%e A401927 For n = 52, its primorial base representation is A049345(52) = '1320', thus the radix primes of nonzero digits are 3, 5 and 7, and a(n) = 3# * 5# * 7# = A034386(3) * A034386(5) * A034386(7) = 6 * 30 * 210 = 37800.
%o A401927 (PARI)
%o A401927 A108951(n) = { my(f=factor(n)); prod(i=1, #f~,  prod(i=1, primepi(f[i, 1]), prime(i))^f[i, 2]); };
%o A401927 A328571(n) = { my(m=1, p=2); while(n, m *= (p^!!(n%p)); n = n\p; p = nextprime(1+p)); (m); };
%o A401927 A401927(n) = A108951(A328571(n));
%o A401927 (PARI) A401927(n) = { my(m=1, p=2); while(n, if(n%p > 0, m *= prod(j=1, primepi(p), prime(j))); n = n\p; p = nextprime(1+p)); (m); };
%Y A401927 Cf. A034386, A007947, A108951, A276086, A328571 [= A319626(a(n))], A328571, A401928 (rgs-transform).
%Y A401927 Cf. also A401926.
%K A401927 nonn,base,easy,new
%O A401927 0,2
%A A401927 _Antti Karttunen_, Nov 20 2026
